---
import Layout from '../../layouts/layout.astro';
---

<Layout>
<div class = "page-layout">
<div class = "text-portion">
<div class = "text">
<h1>Overview: Silicon Cladding for Lightweight Mirrors</h1>
<p>Large-aperture far-ultraviolet (FUV) space telescopes demand extremely low optical surface roughness, but aluminum — the preferred FUV reflector — is difficult to polish directly to that quality; diamond-turned aluminum typically leaves tens to hundreds of angstroms RMS of residual roughness, increasing scatter at short wavelengths. ZeCoat's proprietary silicon cladding technology solves this by depositing a thick, homogeneous layer of high-purity silicon that can be figured and polished with conventional optical techniques, giving aluminum and other lightweight substrates an optical-quality finish. Originally developed under NASA and Department of Defense programs, the process also applies to silicon carbide, metal-composite structures, beryllium, and carbon-based composites that are otherwise difficult to polish directly.
</p>
</div>
<hr>
<div class = "text">
<h1>Abstract &amp; Publications</h1>
<ul class="pub-list">
<li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1117/12.2191445" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Low-Stress Silicon Cladding Made by Pulsed-Ion-Assisted Evaporation — Sheikh, 2015</a></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>SBIR Contracts</strong></p>
<ul class="pub-list">
<li><a href="https://www.sbir.gov/awards/147519" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Low-Stress Silicon Cladding for Surface Finishing Large UVOIR Mirrors — NASA SBIR Phase II, 2014</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.sbir.gov/awards/140978" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Low-Stress Silicon Cladding for Surface Finishing Large UVOIR Mirrors — NASA SBIR Phase I, 2013</a></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div class = "right-column">
<div class = "featured-image-section">
<img src="/SiliconCladding.webp" alt="Silicon Cladding for Lightweight Mirrors" class="featured-image" />
</div>
<h2>Benefits</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Enables optical-quality finishing (&lt;2 &Aring; RMS) of aluminum and other hard-to-polish substrates.</li>
    <li>Low residual stress (&lt;100 MPa) preserves figure on thin, lightweight mirrors.</li>
    <li>Demonstrated on apertures up to 2 meters with &plusmn;2% thickness uniformity.</li>
    <li>Applicable to silicon carbide, beryllium, metal-composite, and carbon-based substrates.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</Layout>

